{"records":[{"id":"cx297hzkjk","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled: \"Endogenous DNA 3' blocks are vulnerabilities for BRCA1 and BRCA2 deficiency and are reversed by the APE2 nuclease\". Alvarez-Quilon et al. 2020","description":"Primary data for the paper entitled \"Endogenous DNA 3' blocks are vulnerabilities for BRCA1 and BRCA2 deficiency and are reversed by the APE2 nuclease\" published in Molecular Cell (DOI: 10.1016/j.molcel.2020.05.021).\n\nThe dataset includes:\n1) Uncropped blot images.\n2) the sgRNA readcounts for the CRISPR screens included in this work.\n3) Primary values used for all graphs included in the manuscript.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-05-31T16:48:57.828Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cx297hzkjk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cx297hzkjk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Alvarez-Quilon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r49yg49ddc","title":"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses. Wang et al.","description":"Supplemental Tables S1 to S5 in Microsoft Excel format for the article \"Genetic screens identify host factors for SARS-CoV-2 and common cold coronaviruses\" (Wang et al., 2020):\nTable S1: CRISPR screen results. MaGECK output for positive gene enrichment analysis of SARS-CoV-2, 229E and OC43 host factor screens. Related to Figure 1. \nTable S2: Gene ontology enrichment analysis of individual CRISPR screens and network propagation clusters. Related to Figure 2.\nTable S3: Network propagation results. Related to Figure 2. \nTable S4: RNP editing efficiencies in A549-ACE2 and Huh7.5.1-ACE2/TMPRSS2 cells. Related to Figures 3 and 5.\nTable S5: Oligonucleotide sequences used in this study. Related to STAR Methods. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-25T18:59:20.104Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r49yg49ddc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r49yg49ddc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Andreas","last_name":"Puschnik","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9xkgn447vz","title":"In vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Summary: Despite remarkable clinical efficacies of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that inhibition of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances tumor immunity and ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. Cop1 inhibition stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the TNBC tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-23T12:28:59.397Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xkgn447vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xkgn447vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Collin","last_name":"Tokheim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binbin","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Protein Degradation","Immunotherapy","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cx67bdkfrg","title":"Accompanying files for STAR Protocol: \"Genome-scale chemogenomic CRISPR screens in human cells using the TKOv3 library\"","description":"Additional files to help implement CRISPR screens with the TKOv3.  This include scripts for trimming sequences and for implementing dark cycles on the NextSeq500 platform. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-12T08:11:30.002Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cx67bdkfrg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cx67bdkfrg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing","Genetic Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":107286,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":99,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":107286,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":106834,"restricted":55,"embargoed":397},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1877},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":559},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":432},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":367},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Ape